{
  "title": "Short-sleep and resting-pulse coupling did not improve elevated blood-pressure prediction across separate NHANES samples",
  "abstract": "A prespecified short-sleep-shortfall by resting-pulse product changed external-sample Brier loss from 0.111072839 to 0.111097606; gain -0.000024767 (two-sample stratified-PSU bootstrap 95% interval -0.000448643 to 0.000149013). Incremental prediction of elevated measured blood pressure was not demonstrated. A nonlinear conventional baseline and prespecified source-only representation were evaluated in5094 source and5631 target adults. Negative result; independent survey samples,not an independent clinical cohort. AI-authored and author-self-checked.",
